%[text] %[text:anchor:T_0E87ADB8] # Man On The Moon
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](file:..\\MainMenu.m)
%[text] Conservation of Mass and Momentum are the fundamental equations of fluid dynamics. This script investigates their application to the modeling of the launch of one of the most famous space exploration missions, comparing the results to the actual flight log from the mission.
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![decorative](text:image:2200)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"} %[text:anchor:M_597CA885]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_51183E7B] **Before you get started:**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BF646A47] This live script is intended to be used with the code hidden. On the **View** tab of the MATLAB Toolstrip, in the **View** section, select **Hide Code**.  Alternately, select **Hide Code** using the icon ![decorative](text:image:11fa) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
%[text] ![Bright idea symbol](text:image:1bfa) Although the code is hidden, some interactivity requires familiarity with MATLAB. If you need more instruction, consider taking [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted), a free 2-hour online tutorial that teaches the essentials of MATLAB.
%[text] The ![Try this icon](text:image:969e)  and  ![Exercise icon](text:image:7f94) icons refer to two different types of interactive activities that you will find in this script. The ![Try this icon](text:image:6893)  usually indicates an interaction where you will explore the visualization of some concept introduced in this script. The ![Exercise icon](text:image:33a3) interactions are designed to challenge your understanding of those concepts and may be used for grading and completion checks by your professor.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_578876F5] ## Introduction
%[text] From 1961 to 1972, NASA pursued the Apollo space exploration program. Named after the Greek god of the sun, the program’s principal objective was to establish the first human presence on the Moon. On July 16th, 1969, the Apollo 14 mission launched from Cape Canaveral, Florida. The mission Commander, Neil Armstrong, would become the first astronaut to set foot on the Moon. In this script, we will use the mass and momentum conservation equations to study the first phase of this mission’s launch.
%[text] The rocket used to send the small, crewed spacecraft into Earth orbit and later on to the Moon was the Saturn V. Until November 2022, this rocket was the most powerful ever used to launch a space exploration mission. The rocket launch sequence is divided into multiple phases; we will study the first phase in this script. 
%[text] In this script, we are using the integral form of the mass and momentum conservation equation to predict the altitude with respect to time during the first phase of the launch. The known parameters of the problem are the rocket's initial mass $m\_0${"altText":"m\_0"}, the rocket specific impulse $I\_{sp}${"altText":"I\_{sp}"}, the rocket burn time $t\_{s1}${"altText":"t\_{s1}"}, and the mass of the fuel $m\_p${"altText":"m\_p"}. The rocket’s specific impulse is a measure of the efficiency of the rocket engine. It can be used to estimate the propellant velocity at the exhaust of the rocket engine using the following relation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_088FA410] $u\_e = g I\_{sp}${"altText":"u\_e = g I\_{sp}"}
%[text] where $u\_e${"altText":"u\_e"} represents the exhaust velocity and $g${"altText":"g"} is the gravity. The exhaust velocity is considered constant during the ascent’s first phase.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0B11F87C] ![Try this icon](text:image:5a52) **Try**. To understand the enormous size of the Saturn V rocket, click the following button to compare its size to an American football field at the same scale.
  %[control:button:2d52]{"position":[1,2]}

DrawSaturn(DrawFootball=true);
title("Saturn V versus Football Field")
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_77C90F83] ## Control Volume and Control Surfaces 
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:482e) **Definition.** In continuum and fluid mechanics, the control volume method allows for solving the integral form of conservation equation (e.g., mass, momentum, and energy). While fluids are deformable and usually do not hold a fixed shape, the control volume method allows studying the rate of change in mass, momentum, and energy within an abstract volume in space.
%[text] **This calculation from this method allows the quick study of the system, such as its change in mass, the forces’ action, the difference in temperature, etc.** The first step of the control volume method involves determining a suitable control volume to study the system.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:39a0) **Try**. We are proposing three different definitions of the Control Volume. Use the following drop-down to explore the proposed definitions.
DrawSaturn(ControlVolume = 1); %[control:dropdown:3a0b]{"position":[28,29]}
title("Control Volume Proposed Definitions")
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_ACEA1734] ![Exercise icon](text:image:2c39) **Exercise.** Which control volumes are the most adequate for this problem?
qControlVolumeDefinition("None") %[control:dropdown:3445]{"position":[26,32]}
%%
%[text] The control volume is defined, and we can now break down its boundary into multiple control surfaces. This option often simplifies the computation.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:208e) **Try**. Explore the definition of the control volume and control surfaces used throughout this script.
DrawSaturn(Highlight="none"); %[control:dropdown:0ea0]{"position":[22,28]}
title("Adopted Control Volume Definition")
%%
%[text] As stated previously, the control volume method is used to solve the integral form of the conservation equations, which include the calculation of [fluxes](internal:M_A5AE3875) through the control surfaces. This step is a common source of error, as it is easy to commit a sign error in the dot product within the integral of the following form:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_{CS} (\\vec{u}\\cdot \\vec{n})\\phi \\; dS${"altText":"\int\_{CS} (\vec{u}\cdot \vec{n})\phi ; dS"} $(\\star)${"altText":"(\star)"}
%[text] In $(\\star)${"altText":"(\star)"}, $\\phi${"altText":"\phi"} represents any physical quantity of interest, which could be the density $\\rho${"altText":"\rho"} for the mass conservation equation or the momentum $\\rho\\vec{u}${"altText":"\rho\vec{u}"} for the momentum conservation equation. The normal unit vector along the control surface is expressed by $\\vec{n}${"altText":"\vec{n}"} . It is defined so that the resulting flux is accounted for positively if it leaves the control volume and negatively if it enters the control volume.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_58D8D98D] ![Exercise icon](text:image:001a) **Exercise.** What is the direction of the normal unit vector to the bottom control surface to satisfy this convention?
qNormalBottomSurface("none") %[control:dropdown:5dce]{"position":[22,28]}
%%
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:411b) **Pro-tip**. Formally defining the normal unit vector of the control surfaces is not an optional step, but it might help you avoid error signs later in the calculation. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A12E264F] ![Try this icon](text:image:1fa9) **Try**. Use the following drop-down control to visualize the normal unit vector over each control surface.
LookAtNormalVector("none") %[control:dropdown:5170]{"position":[20,26]}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_70345BE2] **Remark:** $\\vec{n}${"altText":"\vec{n}"} represents the normal unit vector, therefore $||\\vec{n}|| = 1${"altText":"||\vec{n}|| = 1"}. The normal unit vector will not be up to scale on the previous figure and all following figures.
%[text] **Remark:**  Note that the direction of the normal unit vector along the right and left control surface is not the same along the whole surface. This is because, unlike the bottom control surface, these surfaces are not defined by a straight line.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A3190B37] ## Solve the Mass Conservation Equation
%[text] Now that the control volume, control surfaces, and their normal unit vector are formally defined, we can apply the integral form of the conservation equations over the control volume. Before lift-off, most of the rocket’s mass is composed of the propellant itself. During the launch, the mass of the rocket will vary drastically. We are applying the mass conservation equation to the control volume to study the rocket mass variation with time.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_B71E27BA] ![decorative](text:image:29ea) **Definition.** Integral Form of the Mass Conservation Equation
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_17013B24] $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\int\_{CV}\\rho \\; dV+\\int\_{CS} (\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho \\; dS = 0${"altText":"\displaystyle\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int\_{CV}\rho ; dV+\int\_{CS} (\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho ; dS = 0"}
DrawSaturn(Highlight="CSB", ZoomIn=true, NormalVector=true, DrawExhaustVelocity=true, ...
    WriteExhaustVelocity=true, DrawExhaustMassFlow=true, WriteExhaustMassFlow=true);
annotation('textarrow',Position=[0.7 0.35 0.1 0]);
annotation('textbox',Position=[0.8 0.3 0.2 0.1],String="$\vec{e_x}$",Interpreter="latex",...
    VerticalAlignment="middle",LineStyle="none");
annotation('textarrow',Position=[0.7 0.35 0 0.14]);
annotation('textbox',Position=[0.6 0.46 0.2 0.1],String="$\vec{e_y}$",Interpreter="latex",...
    LineStyle="none",HorizontalAlignment="center");
  %[control:button:3c8a]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FF125724] **Remark:**  In the previous figure, we defined the coordinates system used in the rest of the script.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_31824708] ### **Simplifying Mass Conservation**
%[text] The first step of the calculation is to break the integral over the control surfaces we defined previously.
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_510BDC0F] $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\int\_{CV}\\rho \\; dV+\\int\_{CS} (\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho \\; dS = \\\\\n\n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\int\_{CV}\\rho \\; dV}\_\\text{A}\n+\n\\underbrace{\\int\_{CS\_{right}} (\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho \\; dS }\_\\text{B}\n+\n\\underbrace{\\int\_{CS\_{left}} (\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho \\; dS }\_\\text{C}\n+\n\\underbrace{\\int\_{CS\_{bottom}} (\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho\\;  dS }\_\\text{D}\n= 0${"altText":"\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int\_{CV}\rho ; dV+\int\_{CS} (\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho ; dS =   \underbrace{\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int\_{CV}\rho ; dV}\_\text{A} + \underbrace{\int\_{CS\_{right}} (\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho ; dS }\_\text{B} + \underbrace{\int\_{CS\_{left}} (\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho ; dS }\_\text{C} + \underbrace{\int\_{CS\_{bottom}} (\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho;  dS }\_\text{D} = 0"} $(\\heartsuit)${"altText":"(\heartsuit)"}
%[text] The second step should simplify equation $(\\heartsuit)${"altText":"(\heartsuit)"}, by identifying the terms equal to zero.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_7D76F0E0] ![Exercise icon](text:image:0070) **Exercise.** In our case, identify the term equal to zero, if any.
qNullTermMass(A = false, B = false, C = false, D = false);   %[control:checkbox:1007]{"position":[19,24]} %[control:checkbox:6092]{"position":[30,35]} %[control:checkbox:398e]{"position":[41,46]} %[control:checkbox:41f2]{"position":[52,57]} %[control:button:83e1]{"position":[60,61]}
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_E99249A2] ![decorative](text:image:6c42) **Assumption.** The mass flux through the bottom control surface corresponds to the amount of propellant leaving the rocket engines per unit of time, and it is usually expressed in (kg/s). In our case, we are considering this mass flow to be constant during the first stage of the launch:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\dot{m}\_e = m\_p \\;/ \\;t\_{p1}${"altText":"\dot{m}\_e = m\_p ;\/ ;t\_{p1}"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BC41D32D] ### Calculation of Inertial Term
%[text] Now that the mass conservation equation [$(\\heartsuit)${"altText":"(\heartsuit)"}](internal:M_510BDC0F) has been simplified as much as possible:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\underbrace{\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\int\_{CV}\\rho \\; dV}\_\\text{A}\n+\n\\underbrace{\\int\_{CS\_{bottom}} (\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho\\;  dS }\_\\text{D}\n= 0\n\\quad\\quad(\\heartsuit)${"altText":"\underbrace{\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int\_{CV}\rho ; dV}\_\text{A} + \underbrace{\int\_{CS\_{bottom}} (\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho;  dS }\_\text{D} = 0 \quad\quad(\heartsuit)"}
%[text] It is time to express our first integral, the inertial term of the mass conservation equation. This term corresponds to mass variation per unit of time and should be expressed in units such as (kg/s).
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_98C72091] ![Exercise icon](text:image:3b78) **Exercise.** How can we express term A in $(\\heartsuit)${"altText":"(\heartsuit)"}? where $m(t)${"altText":"m(t)"} corresponds to the rocket's mass with respect to time.
%[text] $\\text{A) } \\frac{\\partial m(t)}{\\partial t} \n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{B) } {\\bf u}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial m(t)}{\\partial t}\n\\quad \\quad\n\\text{C) } u\_e \\frac{\\partial m(t)}{\\partial t}\n\\quad \\quad\n\\text{D) } \\frac{ \\partial (t \\; m(t) )}{\\partial t}${"altText":"\text{A) } \frac{\partial m(t)}{\partial t}  \quad\quad \text{B) } {\bf u}\cdot \frac{\partial m(t)}{\partial t} \quad \quad \text{C) } u\_e \frac{\partial m(t)}{\partial t} \quad \quad \text{D) } \frac{ \partial (t ; m(t) )}{\partial t}"}
qInertiaMass(0); %[control:dropdown:0ea3]{"position":[14,15]}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_23FE8127] ### Calculation of Mass Flux Term
%[text] The remaining integral term in the mass conservation equation [$(\\heartsuit)${"altText":"(\heartsuit)"}](internal:M_510BDC0F) consists of the mass flux through the bottom control surface.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_{CS\_{bottom}} (\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho \\; dS \n${"altText":"\int\_{CS\_{bottom}} (\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho ; dS  "}
DrawSaturn(Highlight="CSB", ZoomIn=true, NormalVector=true, DrawExhaustVelocity=true, WriteExhaustVelocity=true);
title("Rocket bottom with velocity and normal unit vector")
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_81DDF66C] ![Exercise icon](text:image:9cc1) **Exercise.** Considering the direction of $\\vec{n}${"altText":"\vec{n}"} and  $\\vec{u}${"altText":"\vec{u}"}; simplify $\\vec{u} \\cdot \\vec{n}${"altText":"\vec{u} \cdot \vec{n}"},
%[text] $\\text{A) }\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n} = -u\_e\n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{B) }\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n} = u\_e${"altText":"\text{A) }\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n} = -u\_e \quad\quad \text{B) }\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n} = u\_e"}
qDotProductMass(0) %[control:dropdown:631c]{"position":[17,18]}
%%
%[text] With the dot product resolved, it is time to integrate over the control surface.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_{CS\_{bottom}} \\rho u\_e \\; dS\n${"altText":"\int\_{CS\_{bottom}} \rho u\_e ; dS "}
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_3EDD2B31] ![Exercise icon](text:image:3be7) **Exercise.** Express the result of the integral from the previous equation in terms of $\\dot{m}\_e${"altText":"\dot{m}\_e"} (be careful with the units).
%[text] $\\text{A) } \\vec{u}\\cdot\\dot{m}\_e\n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{B) } -u\_e\\dot{m}\_e\n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{C) } \\dot{m}\_e\n${"altText":"\text{A) } \vec{u}\cdot\dot{m}\_e \quad\quad \text{B) } -u\_e\dot{m}\_e \quad\quad \text{C) } \dot{m}\_e "}
qMassFlowTerm(0) %[control:dropdown:59fd]{"position":[15,16]}
%%
DrawSaturn(ZoomIn=true, Highlight="CSB", NormalVector=true, DrawExhaustVelocity=true, WriteExhaustVelocity=true, ...
    DrawExhaustMassFlow=true, WriteExhaustMassFlow=true, WriteMass=true);
title("Summary of Rocket's Mass Balance")
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_177633A9] ### Solve
%[text] The final form of the mass conservation equation [$(\\heartsuit)${"altText":"(\heartsuit)"}](internal:M_510BDC0F) for this rocket model consists of the differential equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_62242C83] $\\underbrace{\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\int\_{CV}\\rho \\; dV}\_\\text{A}\n+\n\\underbrace{\\int\_{CS\_{bottom}} (\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho\\;  dS }\_\\text{D}\n= 0\\\\\n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\partial m(t)}{\\partial t}}\_\\text{A}\n+ \n\\underbrace{\\dot{m}\_e}\_\\text{D}\n = \n0 \\quad\\quad (\\heartsuit)${"altText":"\underbrace{\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int\_{CV}\rho ; dV}\_\text{A} + \underbrace{\int\_{CS\_{bottom}} (\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho;  dS }\_\text{D} = 0 \underbrace{\frac{\partial m(t)}{\partial t}}\_\text{A} +  \underbrace{\dot{m}\_e}\_\text{D}  =  0 \quad\quad (\heartsuit)"}
%[text] The mass conservation equation led to a first-order differential equation. This equation is easy to solve analytically and has a solution of the following form:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $m(t) = -\\dot{m}\_e   t + C${"altText":"m(t) = -\dot{m}\_e   t + C"}
%[text] The integration constant $C${"altText":"C"} can be determined by considering the initial mass of the control volume, $m\_0${"altText":"m\_0"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $m(0) = m\_0 = -\\dot{m}\_e(0)+C${"altText":"m(0) = m\_0 = -\dot{m}\_e(0)+C"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $m(t) = m\_0 - \\dot{m}\_e t${"altText":"m(t) = m\_0 - \dot{m}\_e t"} 
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_3CBE0CC2] The initial mass of the Saturn V used in Apollo 14 was 2,950,000 kg. Out of this mass, around 70% (2,000,000 kg) was the mass of propellant $m\_p${"altText":"m\_p"} burned during the first phase of the launch.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_8F658F3B] ![Try this icon](text:image:8fba) **Try**. Considering the mass of the propellant and the fact that it was entirely burnt during the first phase of the launch, which lasted 161 seconds, set the [mass flow](internal:M_E99249A2) at its right value on the day of the launch to visualize the mass of the rocket with respect to time.
MassFlow = 15000; %[control:slider:203f]{"position":[12,17]}
SolveMassConservationEquation(MassFlow)
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:264f) **Reflect**. The amount of rocket fuel used during the first phase of the launch represents emptying 200 average car tanks every second during the 161 seconds of the first phase, for a total of almost 34,000 cars. 
%[text] **Remark.** Because the mass balance of this problem is relatively simple, one could have expressed the differential equation $(\\heartsuit)${"altText":"(\heartsuit)"} without formally solving the mass conservation equation.
MassFlow = 2000000/161; % Fix the Mass Flow to the right value for the rest of the script
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_914ACB4C] ## Solve the Momentum Conservation Equation
%[text] In the previous section, we solved the mass conservation equation and defined the mass of the rocket with respect to time. In this section, we will consider the conservation of momentum to determine the rocket’s altitude with respect to time.
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:5afb) **Definition.** Integral Form of the Momentum Conservation Equation
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\underbrace{\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\int\_{CV}\\rho\\vec{u} \\; dV}\_\\text{Acceleration}\n+\n\\underbrace{\\int\_{CS}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho\\vec{u} \\; dS}\_\\text{Thrust}\n=\n\\sum\\vec{F}${"altText":"\underbrace{\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int\_{CV}\rho\vec{u} ; dV}\_\text{Acceleration} + \underbrace{\int\_{CS}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho\vec{u} ; dS}\_\text{Thrust} = \sum\vec{F}"}
%[text] where $\\rho${"altText":"\rho"} is the density, $\\vec{u}${"altText":"\vec{u}"}is the velocity vector, the momentum is defined as $\\rho\\vec{u}${"altText":"\rho\vec{u}"}, and $\\vec{F}${"altText":"\vec{F}"}are the external forces acting on the control volume. We are considering the same [parameters](internal:M_3CBE0CC2) as the previous section and the results of the prior section:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $m(t) = m\_0 - \\dot{m}\_e t${"altText":"m(t) = m\_0 - \dot{m}\_e t"}.
%[text] The rocket’s initial mass is defined as $m\_0 =${"altText":"m\_0 ="} 2,950,000 kg, and the exhaust mass flux is $\\dot{m}\_e = m\_p \\;/\\; t\_{p1} =${"altText":"\dot{m}\_e = m\_p ;\/; t\_{p1} ="} 12,400 (kg/s) during the first phase of the launch.
DrawSaturn(Highlight="CSB", ZoomIn=true, NormalVector=true, DrawExhaustVelocity=true,...
    WriteExhaustVelocity=true, DrawExhaustMassFlow=true, WriteExhaustMassFlow=true, WriteMass=true);
title("Summary of Rocket's Mass Balance")
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3A416119] ### **Simplifying Momentum Conservation**
%[text] Similarly to the step we followed to solve the mass conservation equation, the first step in solving the momentum conservation equation is to break down the integral over the control surface into the control surfaces we define in the first section.
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_7C3452EC] $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\int\_{CV}\\rho\\vec{u} \\; dV\n+\n\\int\_{CS}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho\\vec{u} \\; dS\n= \n\\sum \\vec{F}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\int\_{CV}\\rho\\vec{u} \\; dV\n+\n\\int\_{CS\_\\text{right}}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho\\vec{u} \\; dS\n+\n\\int\_{CS\_\\text{left}}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho\\vec{u} \\; dS\n+\n\\int\_{CS\_\\text{bottom}}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho\\vec{u} \\; dS\n= \n\\sum \\vec{F}\\\\\n${"altText":"\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int\_{CV}\rho\vec{u} ; dV + \int\_{CS}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho\vec{u} ; dS =  \sum \vec{F} \frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int\_{CV}\rho\vec{u} ; dV + \int\_{CS\_\text{right}}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho\vec{u} ; dS + \int\_{CS\_\text{left}}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho\vec{u} ; dS + \int\_{CS\_\text{bottom}}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho\vec{u} ; dS =  \sum \vec{F} "} $(\\spadesuit)${"altText":"(\spadesuit)"}
%[text] Again, the second step involves identifying the [fluxes](internal:M_A5AE3875) equal to zero along the different surfaces.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_37C5E3F4] ![Exercise icon](text:image:2ab4) **Exercise**. Identify the fluxes equal to zero in the previous equation $(\\spadesuit)${"altText":"(\spadesuit)"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $A)\\quad\\displaystyle \\int\_{CS\_{\\text{right}}} (\\vec{u} \\cdot  \\vec{n}) \\rho \\vec{u} \\; dS${"altText":"A)\quad\displaystyle \int\_{CS\_{\text{right}}} (\vec{u} \cdot  \vec{n}) \rho \vec{u} ; dS"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $B)\\quad \\displaystyle \\int\_{CS\_{\\text{left}}} (\\vec{u} \\cdot  \\vec{n}) \\rho \\vec{u} \\; dS${"altText":"B)\quad \displaystyle \int\_{CS\_{\text{left}}} (\vec{u} \cdot  \vec{n}) \rho \vec{u} ; dS"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $C)\\quad\\displaystyle \\int\_{CS\_{\\text{bottom}}} (\\vec{u} \\cdot  \\vec{n}) \\rho \\vec{u} \\; dS${"altText":"C)\quad\displaystyle \int\_{CS\_{\text{bottom}}} (\vec{u} \cdot  \vec{n}) \rho \vec{u} ; dS"}
qNullTermMomentum(CSR = true,CSL = false,CSB = false);  %[control:checkbox:414d]{"position":[25,29]} %[control:checkbox:9171]{"position":[36,41]} %[control:checkbox:8329]{"position":[48,53]} %[control:button:734b]{"position":[55,56]}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D881C81D] ### Calculation of the Acceleration Term
%[text] Now that the momentum conservation equation is as simplified as possible, we can start calculating each term one by one, beginning with the acceleration term:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\int\_{CV}\\rho\\vec{u} \\; dV${"altText":"\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int\_{CV}\rho\vec{u} ; dV"}
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:75b9) **Assumption.** The acceleration term is negligible compared to the other term in [$(\\spadesuit)${"altText":"(\spadesuit)"}](internal:M_7C3452EC). This relies on the fact that the momentum variation due to mass displacement is insignificant compared to the thrust generated at the exhaust of the rocket's engine.
%[text] Thus, the equation [$(\\spadesuit)${"altText":"(\spadesuit)"}](internal:M_7C3452EC) reduces to:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_{CS\_\\text{bottom}}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho\\vec{u} \\; dS\n= \n\\sum\\vec{F}\\\\\n${"altText":"\int\_{CS\_\text{bottom}}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho\vec{u} ; dS =  \sum\vec{F} "}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8BC448DC] ### Projection over the Reference Frame
%[text] The mass conservation equation is scalar, while momentum is a vector quantity. Thus, we must project the equation over the vector of our reference frame [$(\\vec{e\_x},\\vec{e\_y})${"altText":"(\vec{e\_x},\vec{e\_y})"}](internal:M_17013B24).
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_{CS\_\\text{bottom}}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{e\_x}) \\; dS = \\sum F\_x \\\\\n\\int\_{CS\_\\text{bottom}}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{e\_y}) \\; dS = \\sum F\_y${"altText":"\int\_{CS\_\text{bottom}}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{e\_x}) ; dS = \sum F\_x  \int\_{CS\_\text{bottom}}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{e\_y}) ; dS = \sum F\_y"}
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:2141) **Assumption.** As a first approximation, we consider that the rocket's flight trajectory is directly vertical with no horizontal displacement. Therefore, the remaining equation is solely along the y-axis of the domain.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_{CS\_\\text{bottom}}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{e\_y}) \\; dS = \\sum{F\_y}\n\\quad\\quad(\\spadesuit)${"altText":"\int\_{CS\_\text{bottom}}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{e\_y}) ; dS = \sum{F\_y} \quad\quad(\spadesuit)"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2C552B7B] ### Thrust Term
%[text] Next, we calculate the integral term corresponding to the thrust generated by the propellant flow through the rocket's engine. We can predict the resulting sign from the integral to avoid sign error. If the thrust is aligned with the reference frame unit vector [$(\\vec{e\_y})${"altText":"(\vec{e\_y})"}](internal:M_17013B24), then the result of the integral should be positive. Otherwise, it should be negative.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_CFD4074D] ![Exercise icon](text:image:21e0) **Exercise.** In the case of Saturn V, what should be the direction of the thrust force resulting from the mass flux through the rocket's engines?
qThrustDirection( "None") %[control:dropdown:9650]{"position":[19,25]}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_4AAB04A2] ![Exercise icon](text:image:5b47) **Exercise.** What sign results from the two dot product in $\\displaystyle \\int\_{CS\_\\text{bottom}}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{e\_y}) \\; dS \n=\n\\int\_{CS\_\\text{bottom}} \\pm u\_e \\rho u\_e \\; dS\n${"altText":"\displaystyle \int\_{CS\_\text{bottom}}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{e\_y}) ; dS  = \int\_{CS\_\text{bottom}} \pm u\_e \rho u\_e ; dS "}? Is it zero, positive or negative?
qThrustSign(0) %[control:dropdown:593c]{"position":[13,14]}
%%
%[text] Remember that we consider that the [mass flow is constant](internal:M_E99249A2), and the rocket’s [specific impulse](internal:M_088FA410) defines the exhaust velocity during the first phase of the launch.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_ECE7F274] ![Exercise icon](text:image:4b48) **Exercise.** How can $\\displaystyle \\int\_{CS\_\\text{bottom}}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{e\_y}) \\; dS${"altText":"\displaystyle \int\_{CS\_\text{bottom}}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{e\_y}) ; dS"} be expressed in terms of $u\_e${"altText":"u\_e"} and  $\\dot{m}\_e${"altText":"\dot{m}\_e"}?
%[text] $\\text{A) } u\_e^2\\dot{m}\_e\n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{B) } -u\_e\\dot{m}\_e\n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{C) } -\\dot{m}\_e\n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{D) } -u\_e^2${"altText":"\text{A) } u\_e^2\dot{m}\_e \quad\quad \text{B) } -u\_e\dot{m}\_e \quad\quad \text{C) } -\dot{m}\_e \quad\quad \text{D) } -u\_e^2"}
qThrustTerm("None") %[control:dropdown:16b8]{"position":[13,19]}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A3E9792B] ### Forces term
%[text] The last term to be defined in $(\\spadesuit)${"altText":"(\spadesuit)"} is $\\sum F\_y${"altText":"\sum F\_y"}. To calculate this term, we first need to consider what forces are even acting on the control volume?
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_A9212154] ![Exercise icon](text:image:061e) **Exercise.** In the case of Saturn V, what forces should be included?
qBodyForces( ...
    Gravity = false, ... %[control:checkbox:3ff3]{"position":[15,20]}
    Spring = false, ... %[control:checkbox:9657]{"position":[14,19]}
    Electric = false, ... %[control:checkbox:1598]{"position":[16,21]}
    Friction = false, ... %[control:checkbox:3444]{"position":[16,21]}
    Magnetic = false, ... %[control:checkbox:418e]{"position":[16,21]}
    Thrust = false, ... %[control:checkbox:4050]{"position":[14,19]}
    Acceleration = false);  %[control:checkbox:2c80]{"position":[20,25]} %[control:button:20a4]{"position":[27,28]}
%%
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:782b) **Assumption.** We will now neglect the air resistance on the rocket's hull and consider the gravity constant equal to the gravity at sea level.
%[text] The different components of the force term $\\sum F\_y${"altText":"\sum F\_y"} should be positive if their corresponding force is in the same direction as the reference vector [$(\\vec{e\_y})${"altText":"(\vec{e\_y})"}](internal:M_B71E27BA), and negative if in the opposite direction. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_A59F6CE4] ![Exercise icon](text:image:9d48) **Exercise**. What is the correct expression for the gravitational forces acting on the rocket?
%[text] $\\text{A) } g\\,m\_0\n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{B) } -g\\,m\_0 \n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{C) } g \\,\\dot{m}\_e\n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{D) } -g\\,m(t)${"altText":"\text{A) } g,m\_0 \quad\quad \text{B) } -g,m\_0  \quad\quad \text{C) } g ,\dot{m}\_e \quad\quad \text{D) } -g,m(t)"}
qGravityTerm(0) %[control:dropdown:0519]{"position":[14,15]}
%%
%[text] With the gravity being calculated, we are left with the acceleration term. We can think of the acceleration force felt by the control volume, like the force acting on us in a plane at take-off. When the pilot pushes the jet engine at max thrust, we feel a resulting force going into the back of our seat.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_72617483] ![Exercise icon](text:image:60d7) **Exercise**. What should be the direction of the resulting force from the acceleration of the control volume?
qAccelerationDirection("None") %[control:dropdown:882b]{"position":[24,30]}
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:1909) **Exercise**. How can you model the force due to the acceleration of the control volume? In this expression, $y\_r${"altText":"y\_r"} represents the altitude of the control volume; it is attached to the bottom of the rocket so that $y\_r(t=0) = 0${"altText":"y\_r(t=0) = 0"}, the initial elevation of the rocket is 0.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_DEB64B2C] $\\text{A) } m(t)\\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2}\n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{B) } -m(t)\\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2}\n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{C) } \\dot{m}\_e\\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2}\n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{D) } -\\dot{m}\_e\\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2}${"altText":"\text{A) } m(t)\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2} \quad\quad \text{B) } -m(t)\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2} \quad\quad \text{C) } \dot{m}\_e\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2} \quad\quad \text{D) } -\dot{m}\_e\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2}"}
qAccelerationTerm(0) %[control:dropdown:954d]{"position":[19,20]}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6F5649FD] ### Solve
%[text] In the previous subsections, we calculated the term resulting from the momentum conservation equation and found the following equation.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_{CS\_\\text{bottom}}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{e\_y}) \\; dS = \\sum\\vec{F\_y}\n\\quad\\quad(\\spadesuit) \\\\\n-u\_e\\dot{m}\_e = -m(t)\\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2}-m(t)g\\\\\n\\text{Thus, } \\quad \\frac{d^2 y\_r}{dt^2}= \\frac{u\_e\\dot{m}\_e}{m(t)}-g${"altText":"\int\_{CS\_\text{bottom}}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{e\_y}) ; dS = \sum\vec{F\_y} \quad\quad(\spadesuit)  -u\_e\dot{m}\_e = -m(t)\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2}-m(t)g \text{Thus, } \quad \frac{d^2 y\_r}{dt^2}= \frac{u\_e\dot{m}\_e}{m(t)}-g"}
%[text] This latest equation was obtained by considering a constant mass flow and exhaust gas velocity at the rocket engine, neglecting the friction with the surrounding air, having constant gravity, and Saturn V’s purely vertical flight path. Remember that the mass conservation equation result defined the mass of the rocket with respect to time:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $m(t) = m\_0-\\dot{m}\_et${"altText":"m(t) = m\_0-\dot{m}\_et"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_900A246C] ![decorative](text:image:4cc9)**Computational Thinking.** While the resulting mass conservation equation was easy to solve analytically, the second-order differential equation arising from the momentum conservation equation is much more complex. It would be nearly impossible to solve without some of the assumptions we made earlier in this script. It is a perfect candidate for MATLAB's Ordinary Differential Equations solver. The following results from the differential equation were obtained using [`ode45`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/ode45.html?searchHighlight=ode45&s_tid=srchtitle_ode45_1).
  %[control:button:3b22]{"position":[1,2]}
try
    SolveMomentum(MassFlow,false,false)
catch
    warning("MassFlow was not defined, current model is running with mass flow of 12,500 kg/s. "...
        + "Please, solve mass conservation equation to define MassFlow correctly.")
    MassFlow = 12500;
    SolveMomentum(MassFlow,false,false)
end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9A951E43] ![decorative](text:image:0384) **Reflect**. Calculations based on the mass conservation equation allowed us to define the rocket's mass with respect to time. In contrast, the momentum conservation equation analysis gave us the rocket's altitude with respect to time.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A0EAC600] ## Analysis of the model
%[text] The result from $(\\spadesuit)${"altText":"(\spadesuit)"} generally agrees with the Apollo 14 flight log flight data. However, we are overestimating the rocket’s elevation by the end of the first phase of the flight.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_C9632FB3] ![Exercise icon](text:image:2f1a) **Exercise.** Which of the following statements could have caused the inaccuracy of the mathematical model we developed in the previous section?
qSourceInaccuracy( ...
    BadPhysics = false, ... %[control:checkbox:0720]{"position":[18,23]}
    Friction = false, ... %[control:checkbox:10d6]{"position":[16,21]}
    FlightPath = false, ... %[control:checkbox:47e2]{"position":[18,23]}
    Gravity = false, ... %[control:checkbox:5579]{"position":[15,20]}
    Performance = false);   %[control:checkbox:4cf2]{"position":[19,24]} %[control:button:7d73]{"position":[27,28]}
%[text] We will correct some of these inaccuracies in the remaining parts of the script. First, we will consider the gravity variation with respect to the altitude; second, we will consider the rocket's flight path.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0F267C50] ### **Correction for Gravity**
%[text] The gravity felt by the rocket is a function of the altitude according to the following relation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $g(y\_r) = \\frac{GM\_t}{(r\_t+y\_r)^2}${"altText":"g(y\_r) = \frac{GM\_t}{(r\_t+y\_r)^2}"}
%[text] Where $G${"altText":"G"} is the gravitational constant, $M\_t${"altText":"M\_t"} is the Earth's mass ($5.97\\times10^{24}${"altText":"5.97\times10^{24}"} kg), $r\_t${"altText":"r\_t"} is the Earth's radius ($6.371\\times10^{6}${"altText":"6.371\times10^{6}"} m) and $y\_r${"altText":"y\_r"} is the rocket’s elevation as defined previously.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{d^2 y\_r}{dt^2}= \\frac{u\_e\\dot{m}\_e}{m(t)}-g(y\_r)${"altText":"\frac{d^2 y\_r}{dt^2}= \frac{u\_e\dot{m}\_e}{m(t)}-g(y\_r)"}
%[text] The new differential equation is solved using the [same method](internal:M_900A246C) as the first model.
  %[control:button:03aa]{"position":[1,2]}
try
    SolveMomentum(MassFlow,true,false)
catch
    warning("MassFlow was not defined, current model is running with mass flow of 12,500 kg/s. "...
        + "Please, solve mass conservation equation to define MassFlow correctly.")
    MassFlow = 12500;
    SolveMomentum(MassFlow,true,false)
end
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:057c) **Reflect**. Because gravity decreases with altitude, we should expect the rocket to reach a higher altitude by adding this variation to our model. However, the difference with the first results seems minor during the first phase of the launch. This is because the rocket "only" reaches around 60 km altitude. Its effect would be much more important as altitude grows.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2EB484CF] ### Correction for rocket flight path angle
%[text] In the previous section, we consider that the thrust of the rocket was directed vertically; therefore, the rocket’s flight path should be purely vertical. However, the flight path of Apollo 14 was not directed directly vertically. The mission involved getting the spacecraft into Earth orbit for a few revolutions before starting the translunar injection.
  %[control:button:873b]{"position":[1,2]}
DrawSaturn(PathAngle=20);
%%
%[text] Going back to the equation from the mass conservation equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_{CS\_\\text{bottom}}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{e\_x}) \\; dS = \\sum F\_x \\\\\n\\int\_{CS\_\\text{bottom}}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{e\_y}) \\; dS = \\sum F\_y${"altText":"\int\_{CS\_\text{bottom}}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{e\_x}) ; dS = \sum F\_x  \int\_{CS\_\text{bottom}}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{e\_y}) ; dS = \sum F\_y"}
%[text] Even though we no longer consider a purely vertical rocket path, we are only interested in the rocket's altitude. Therefore, our effort is only focusing on the second equation. Developing the y-component of the forces vector, we obtain the following:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_{CS\_\\text{bottom}}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{e\_y}) \\; dS \n= \n-m(t)g(y\_r)-m(t)\\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2}\n${"altText":"\int\_{CS\_\text{bottom}}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{e\_y}) ; dS  =  -m(t)g(y\_r)-m(t)\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2} "}
%[text] The right-hand side of the equation is unchanged from the previous model. However, the left side should differ because the velocity vector $\\vec{u}${"altText":"\vec{u}"} no longer aligns with $\\vec{e\_y}${"altText":"\vec{e\_y}"}.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_90F0B75A] ![Exercise icon](text:image:82a6) **Exercise.** What is the result of the dot product $\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{e\_y}${"altText":"\vec{u}\cdot\vec{e\_y}"}?
%[text] $\\text{A) } \\sin(\\alpha)\\, u\_e\n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{B) } \\frac{u\_e}{\\sin(\\alpha)}\n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{C) } \\cos(\\alpha)\\,u\_e\n\\quad\\quad\n\\text{D) }  \\frac{u\_e}{\\cos(\\alpha)}${"altText":"\text{A) } \sin(\alpha), u\_e \quad\quad \text{B) } \frac{u\_e}{\sin(\alpha)} \quad\quad \text{C) } \cos(\alpha),u\_e \quad\quad \text{D) }  \frac{u\_e}{\cos(\alpha)}"}
qTrigFunction(0) %[control:dropdown:417d]{"position":[15,16]}
%%
%[text]{"align":"center"} Hence, the previous equation, $\\displaystyle \\int\_{CS\_\\text{bottom}}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{e\_y}) \\; dS \n= \n-m(t)g(y\_r)-m(t)\\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2}\n${"altText":"\displaystyle \int\_{CS\_\text{bottom}}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})\rho(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{e\_y}) ; dS  =  -m(t)g(y\_r)-m(t)\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2} "}
%[text]{"align":"center"} turns into:     $-\\cos(\\alpha(t))\\,\\dot{m}\_eu\_e\n= \n-m(t)g(y\_r)-m(t)\\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2}\n${"altText":"-\cos(\alpha(t)),\dot{m}\_eu\_e =  -m(t)g(y\_r)-m(t)\frac{d^2y\_r}{dt^2} "}
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_C84F0D7D] thus,     $\\frac{\\partial^2y(t)}{\\partial t^2}=\\cos(\\alpha(t))\\frac{\\dot{m}\_e u\_e}{m\_o-\\dot{m}\_e t} - g${"altText":"\frac{\partial^2y(t)}{\partial t^2}=\cos(\alpha(t))\frac{\dot{m}\_e u\_e}{m\_o-\dot{m}\_e t} - g"}
%[text] Considering the flight path and the varying gravity, this new differential equation is solved using the [same method](internal:M_900A246C) as the previous models.
  %[control:button:5cd2]{"position":[1,2]}
try
    SolveMomentum(MassFlow,true,true)
catch
    warning("MassFlow was not defined, current model is running with mass flow of 12,500 kg/s. "...
        + "Please, solve mass conservation equation to define MassFlow correctly.")
    MassFlow = 12500;
    SolveMomentum(MassFlow,true,true)
end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_91A53FD6] ![decorative](text:image:6464) **Reflect**. While considering the rocket's real flight path, the model's accuracy improved greatly.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_EE30763B] ## Further exploration
%[text] Check out this [article](https://www.mathworks.com/company/mathworks-stories/nasa-artemis-program-develops-orion-spacecraft-and-sls-rocket-for-the-moon.html) to read more about the development of the next generation of lunar missions.
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:45b6) **Reflect**. The first-order differential equation arising from the mass conservation equation $(\\heartsuit)${"altText":"(\heartsuit)"} was simple enough to solve by hand. However, the second-order differential equation was more complex. Particularly when adding the $\\cos(\\alpha)${"altText":"\cos(\alpha)"} term in $(\\spadesuit)${"altText":"(\spadesuit)"} made it nearly impossible to solve by hand. This case is perfect for using MATLAB's [ordinary differential equations solver](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ordinary-differential-equations.html?s_tid=CRUX_lftnav). In this script, we selected [`ode45`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/ode45.html) to solve $(\\spadesuit)${"altText":"(\spadesuit)"}. The following code example shows how we solve this equation; more details can be found in the definition of [`SolveMomentum(me, gravity, angle)`](internal:M_BFEA5DEB)
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] % Define the differential equation resulting from momentum cons. eq
%[text] ddydt = @(t,y) cosd(alpha(t)).*ue*me./m(t)-g(y);
%[text] dfun = @(t,y) [y(2); ddydt(t,y(1))];
%[text]     
%[text] % Solve the differential equation:
%[text] [t,y] = ode45(dfun, [0 tf], [0; 0]);
%[text] ```
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_69719909] ![decorative](text:image:8995) **Reflect**. In this script, we followed the first stage of the Saturn V launch. Use the button to visualize the spacecraft trajectory from lift-off to the translunar injection. Depending on your platform and network, this graphic might take a few minutes to load.
if false %[control:statebutton:28a1]{"position":[4,9]}
    ShowRocketPath %#ok<UNRCH>
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FD9E0D5F] ## Glossary
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_571CA67D] ![decorative](text:image:3f2b) **Control Volume** is an abstract volume in space that is usually defined to solve the integral form of a conservation equation. This calculation is often called the control volume method used in continuum and fluid mechanics. For a three-dimensional problem, the control volume refers to an abstract volume; for a two-dimensional problem, the control volume refers to an abstract surface.
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:137a) **Control Surface** is the closed boundary surrounding a [control volume](internal:M_571CA67D). With a three-dimensional problem, the control surface refers to an abstract surface, while for a two-dimensional problem, the surface refers to an abstract line. It is often divided into multiple surfaces to simplify the computation.
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:25e9) **Conservation Equations** usually study the rate of change of physical, measurable quantities such as mass, momentum, and energy. For example, let's consider a physically measurable quantity,  the number of people going to see a game. We can also define the density of people $n${"altText":"n"}, the number of people per unit of volume $n=N/V${"altText":"n=N\/V"}. We can picture that the number density of people is not uniform in the stadium (e.g., it is usually more crowded in the stands than in the suites). Moreover, people move during games; some might leave or even get there late for kick-off. Finally, we can safely assume that nobody will be born nor die in the stadium during the game, so the constitutive equation of the problem is the following:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{dN}{dt} =0 \\quad\\quad (\\clubsuit)${"altText":"\frac{dN}{dt} =0 \quad\quad (\clubsuit)"}
%[text] Now, we can consider the stadium as our control volume and its boundary as our control surface. We can apply the Reynolds Transport theorem to express the local change in density.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{d}{dt} \\int\_{CV} n\\;dV = \\int\_{CV}\\frac{\\partial n}{\\partial t}\\;dV+\\int\_{CS}  (\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})n\\;dS${"altText":"\frac{d}{dt} \int\_{CV} n;dV = \int\_{CV}\frac{\partial n}{\partial t};dV+\int\_{CS}  (\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})n;dS"}
%[text] In this case, we must be careful with our notation $n\\neq\\vec{n}${"altText":"n\neq\vec{n}"}, $n${"altText":"n"} is the density of people, while $\\vec{n}${"altText":"\vec{n}"} is the normal unit vector attached to the control surfaces. Integrating the left side of the equation and considering $(\\clubsuit)${"altText":"(\clubsuit)"},
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{d}{dt}\\int\_{CV} n\\;dV = \\frac{d}{dt}(N)=\\frac{dN}{dt} =0${"altText":"\frac{d}{dt}\int\_{CV} n;dV = \frac{d}{dt}(N)=\frac{dN}{dt} =0"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} Therefore, $\\displaystyle\\int\_{CV}\\frac{\\partial n}{\\partial t}\\;dV+\\int\_{CS}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})n\\;dS=0${"altText":"\displaystyle\int\_{CV}\frac{\partial n}{\partial t};dV+\int\_{CS}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})n;dS=0"}
%[text] This latest equation is conserving the number of people in the stadium. Considering that $CV${"altText":"CV"} is the stadium and $CS${"altText":"CS"} its boundary, we can assume that people can only enter and leave the stadium through its gates. Therefore,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\displaystyle\\int\_\\text{Stadium}\\frac{\\partial n}{\\partial t}\\;dV+\\int\_\\text{Gates}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})n\\;dS=0${"altText":"\displaystyle\int\_\text{Stadium}\frac{\partial n}{\partial t};dV+\int\_\text{Gates}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})n;dS=0"}
%[text] We could define another conservation equation modeling the fans’ happiness level. The constitutive equation for $h${"altText":"h"} would look like this:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{dh}{dt} = f(\\text{Score})${"altText":"\frac{dh}{dt} = f(\text{Score})"}
%[text] The stadium fans’ mood variation should be a function of the game’s score. We can follow the same calculation to define a conservation equation of happiness level in the stadium:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\displaystyle\\int\_\\text{Stadium}\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial t}\\;dV+\\int\_\\text{Gates}(\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})h\\;dS=f(Score)${"altText":"\displaystyle\int\_\text{Stadium}\frac{\partial h}{\partial t};dV+\int\_\text{Gates}(\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})h;dS=f(Score)"}
%[text] In non-mathematical terms, this equation tells us that the level of happiness in the stadium varies with the satisfaction of leaving or entering the stadium and the game’s score.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_A5AE3875] ![decorative](text:image:1c8e) **Fluxes** are "things going through something"; now, we must be careful about what those things are and what this something is. In the previous definition of conservation equation, the example of a stadium, we see fluxes of people through the stadium gate.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_\\text{Gates} (\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})n\\;dS${"altText":"\int\_\text{Gates} (\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})n;dS"}
%[text] $\\vec{u}${"altText":"\vec{u}"} is the walking velocity of the people walking in or out of the stadium, $\\vec{n}${"altText":"\vec{n}"} is the normal unit vector attached to the stadium gates, by convention, it is oriented outward of the stadium, and $n${"altText":"n"} is the density of people walking at the stadium entrance.
%[text] If people are walking inside the stadium at a velocity  $u${"altText":"u"},         $\\displaystyle \\int\_\\text{Gates} (\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})n\\;dS \n=\n\\int\_\\text{Gates} -un\\;dS${"altText":"\displaystyle \int\_\text{Gates} (\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})n;dS  = \int\_\text{Gates} -un;dS"}
%[text] If people are stepping outside the stadium at a velocity  $u${"altText":"u"},     $\\displaystyle \\int\_\\text{Gates} (\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})n\\;dS \n=\n\\int\_\\text{Gates} un\\;dS${"altText":"\displaystyle \int\_\text{Gates} (\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})n;dS  = \int\_\text{Gates} un;dS"}
%[text] The results from the integral would be expressed in the number of people per unit of time and could be defined $\\dot{N}\_\\text{Gates} = \\displaystyle \\int\_\\text{Gates} (\\vec{u}\\cdot\\vec{n})n\\;dS ${"altText":"\dot{N}\_\text{Gates} = \displaystyle \int\_\text{Gates} (\vec{u}\cdot\vec{n})n;dS "}.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F797CACB] ## Reference
